{{also|Dido|ɗiɗo|DIDO}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|en}}. The "trick" sense might come from the trick of [[Dido]], queen of Carthage, who, having bought as much land as a hide would cover, is said to have cut it into thin strips long enough to enclose a spot for a citadel.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdaɪdəʊ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪdəʊ|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|didoes}}

# {{lb|en|slang|regional}} A [[fuss]], a [[row]].
#* '''1928''', {{w|Eugene O'Neill}}, ''Strange Interlude'', Act Four, page 139,
#*: … she always had strong physical attraction for me … that time I kissed her … one reason I’ve steered clear since … take no chances on emotional '''didos''' …
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author=w:GB Edwards|title=The Book of Ebenezer Le Page|page=30|year_published=2007|location=New York
|passage=I remember Raymond telling me years later how when he lived at home, if his mother heard he had been seen as much as talking to a girl, she would kick up a '''dido'''.}}
# A [[shrewd]] [[trick]]; an [[antic]]; a [[caper]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author=Joseph Clay Neal|title=Charcoal Sketches; Or, Scenes in a Metropolis|pageurl=http://books.google.ru/books?id=Y8Tgvsg3vq4C&dq=cut%20%22didoes%22&pg=PA201#v=onepage&q&f=false|page=201
|passage=Young people," interposed a passing official, "if you keep a cutting '''didoes''', I must talk to you both like a [[dutch uncle|Dutch uncle]].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1969|author=w:Maya Angelou|title=w:I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings|url=https://archive.org/details/iknowwhycagedbi000maya|chapter=10|page=55|publisher=Bantam|year_published=1971|location=New York
|passage=Our youngest uncle, Billy, was not old enough to join in their '''didoes'''. One of their more flamboyant escapades has become a proud family legend.}}

